{% extends "ap2_en.j2" %}

{% block summary %}
<p>The OSLO Organization application profile is a profile on existing vocabulary regarding organizations including the <a href="https://data.vlaanderen.be/ns/organisatie">OSLO Organization vocabulary</a>.</p><p>Normally, an application profile brings together terms needed for a particular application (including additional constraints such as cardinalities and code lists). In this case, however, we do not have a specific application in mind, i.e. this is a basic profile. It indicates which terms within OSLO are considered relevant for the exchange of information about organizations.</p><p>This means that it must be possible to describe organizations, but also registered organizations and certainly public organizations. It must also be possible to exchange information about the branches of an organization. Attention has also been paid to the relationship of individuals (and organizations) with an organization, whether or not in a certain capacity. Finally, it must also be possible to document the life course of an organisation, from establishment to discontinuation and the possible succession of organisations over time (e.g. through merger).</p><p>The cardinalities are not very restrictive, only the absolute minimum is mandatory. However, mandatory attributes are added for certain subclasses, e.g. the registration number for registered organizations is mandatory, for public organizations a change event of the type of establishment is always necessary.</p><p>The values of the code lists used have largely yet to be determined.</p>
{% endblock %}

{% block status %}
<p>This application profile serves to clarify the specific use of the entities relevant to the described application.</p>
<p>This document was published as a Candidate Standard. The specification described in this document is currently undergoing a period of public review, during which no changes will be made to this specification.<br/>This document is intended to become a standard in the described domain. Nevertheless, it does not currently have an official statute and does not represent a consensus within OSLO.</p>
<p>Feedback on this specification can be given as a topic in our <a href="https://github.com/Informatievlaanderen/OSLO-Public-Discussion">public review discussion list</a>.</p>
{% endblock %}

{% block conformance %}
<p>An application profile is a data exchange specification that introduces additional restrictions on the application of vocabularies.
Such additional restrictions may include the following elements:</p>
<p>
<ul>
<li>refinement of the terminology (classes and properties) consistent with the semantics of the relevant specifications with a specific use as a goal;</li>
<li>External terminology (classes and properties) used for new/additional terms that do not exist in the existing vocabularies.</li>
</ul>
</p>
<p>To be compliant with this application profile, an implementation must:</p>
<p>
<ul>
<li>MUST always contain the properties that have a minimum cardinality of 1 for each class.</li>
<li>MUST NOT contain more than 1 instance of traits that have 1 as the maximum cardinality.</li>
<li>MAG uses terminology (classes and properties) in a way that is consistent with its semantics (definition, use, domain, and scope).</li>
<li>MUST NOT use terminology from controlled vocabularies other than that defined in this application profile.</li>
<li>MAG can be extended with classes and properties of other data models (vocabularies) that do not overlap with terminology from this application profile.</li>
</ul>
</p>  
{% endblock %}

